    "obs_and_metrics": [],
    "innovation": "This paper presents a comprehensive meta-analysis for the OSPAR Maritime Area, demonstrating that observed changes in the distribution and abundance of marine species\u2014including zooplankton, benthos, and fish\u2014are largely consistent with the expected effects of regional climate change.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Atlantic Multidecadal Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Temporal patterns of temperature change also reveal a systematic spatial structure at the subregional level (Figure 2.1.3). The two longest instrumentally recorded time-series of temperature data in the Northeast Atlantic (Kola section and Faroe-Shetland Channel) reveal strong coherence at time-scales and periodicities greater than the decadal scale (Figures 2.1.4 and 2.1.5). The Kola and Faroe\u2013Shetland data reflect ocean-water mass conditions and are closely related to the Atlantic Multidecadal Oscillation (AMO) index (Sutton and Hodson, 2005; Figure 2.1.6). The North Atlantic is currently in a positive phase of the AMO.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Subpolar Gyre",
            "source_entity_type": "Ocean_Circulation",
            "relation": "OBSERVED_IN_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "A regional scale of natural variability in the North Atlantic is connected to changes in the Subpolar Gyre (H\u00e4kkinen and Rhines, 2004). The weakening of the Subpolar Gyre after 1995 has been demonstrated to have a large effect on hydrographic conditions in the eastern part of the OSPAR area as a result of the presence of a larger fraction of warmer and more saline water from the eastern Atlantic (Hatun et al., 2005; Figure 2.1.4).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "North Atlantic Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Nordic Seas",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Since the 1960s, changes in the large-scale wind pattern, principally the North Atlantic Oscillation (NAO), have resulted in a gradual change in the water mass distribution in the Nordic Seas. In particular, this is manifested by the development of a layer of Arctic intermediate waters, deriving from the Greenland and Iceland seas and spreading over the entire Norwegian Sea (Blindheim et al., 2000).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "North Atlantic Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "OSPAR regions",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Superimposed on the AMO signal are the interannual to decadal-scale temperature variations that are largely linked to the NAO. Although the AMO is a thermal signal, the NAO causes changes in circulation and volume fluxes (Sundby and Drinkwater, 2007), as well as in the thermal regime. The NAO signal also has a smaller spatial scale, resulting in different development and signature in the southern and northern OSPAR regions.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "North Atlantic Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "La Coru\u00f1a",
            "target_entity_type": "City",
            "paragraph": "The distribution of many benthic species in the Bay of Biscay, including macroalgae, molluscs, and arthropods, has been studied since the end of the 19th century. Some latitudinal shifts in distribution, both northwards and southwards, have been documented and are related to the occurrence of warm and cool periods during the 20th century (Alcock, 2003). Similarly, changes in the benthic community off La Coru\u00f1a (Spain) could be partly explained in terms of the expansion and contraction of warm and cold-water species in response to changing environmental conditions linked to the NAO (Lopez-Jamar et al., 1995; S. Parra, pers. comm.).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "North Atlantic Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Sea",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The southward shift of warm-tolerant species in the North Sea is consistent with climate change acting through: \u2022 the warming and increasing availability of shallow habitats in the southern North Sea; \u2022 the NAO-linked inflows of warm water into the northeastern North Sea.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "North Atlantic Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Orkney",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The survival of northern fulmars breeding on Eynhallow, Orkney, UK, was negatively correlated with the winter North Atlantic Oscillation index one year earlier (Grosbois and Thompson, 2005). Thompson and Ollason (2001) investigated the survival/increased population breeding performance over time in fulmar populations, and proposed that warmer conditions favoured increased abundance.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Arctic Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Svalbard",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "A decline in reproductive output and body mass of the polar bear (U. maritimus) in Svalbard, Norway, between 1988 and 2002, is linked to both large-scale climatic variation (Arctic Oscillation index) and upper trophic level changes in the Arctic marine ecosystem. However, changes could also be the result of an increase in population abundance in the area.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "North Atlantic Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "OSPAR Maritime Area",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Within the OSPAR Maritime Area, long-term changes in large-scale distribution in the bottlenose dolphin (T. truncatus), common dolphin (Delphinus delphis), and white-beaked dolphin (Lagenorhychus albirostris) populations over the past 100 years seem to have occurred. These may be a result of changes in SST (and linked with changes in the NAO index).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        }
    ],
